When tail-biting is adopted, the code trellis can be viewed as a circle (see figure below): the iterative decoding of such codes involves repeated and continuous turns around the circular trellis. The number of turns performed is equal to the required number of iterations. The state probabilities or metrics, according to the chosen decoding algorithm, computed at the end of each turn are used as initial values for the next turn. For instance, if the MAP, Log-MAP or Max-Log-MAP soft-in soft-out decoding algorithm is used, the final forward (respectively backward) state probabilities or metrics computed at the end of each iteration are used as initial values for the next iteration. The decoding process can start anywhere in the circular trellis. At the first iteration, the forward and backward processes can be initialized with equiprobable values.

