In RAN#122 meeting, the following agreement was reached: Agreement On enhanced overall coverage, identify coverage target(s) considering diverse use cases and device types Antenna gain model and different metrics as following have been discussed to evaluate the coverage performance in NR Rel-17 NR coverage enhancement, and captured in TR 38.830. gNB antenna gain modelling For TDL channel model, the gain of antenna gain component 2 is expressed by: Antenna gain component 2 = 10 * log 10(N/k)-1, where 1 is a correction factor to account for various non-idealities impacting the actual gain of antenna gain component 2, and reported by companies. Figure 5: gNB antenna gain modelling for TDL channel model For CDL channel model, antenna gain component 2 = 0. Figure 6: gNB antenna gain modelling for CDL channel model For TDL channel model and CDL channel model, the gain of antenna gain components 3 and 4 is expressed by: Antenna gain component 3 + Antenna gain component 4 = Antenna Element Gain + 10 * log 10(M/N) -2. where 2 is a correction factor to account for various non-idealities impacting the actual gain of antenna gain component 3, and reported by companies. UE antenna gain modelling UE antenna gain is expressed by: UE antenna gain = Antenna Element Gain + 10 * log 10(M/k) -3. where k is the number of Tx/Rx chains, e.g., number of SRS/CSI-RS ports to be simulated in LLS and M is the number of antenna elements used for both transmission and reception, i.e., M/2 xpol antenna elements. 3 is a correction factor to account for various non-idealities impacting the actual antenna array gain, and can be reported by companies. Proposal 13: Antenna gain modeling in TR 38.830 can be the start point for the antenna gain modeling study in 6G Coverage. Performance metrics Three metrics are defined in Rel-17 Coverage Enhancement SI to evaluate the coverage performance, and also applied in subsequent technologies, e.g., RedCap, LP-WUS, etc. Definition of MCL (Maximum Path Loss): - MCL = Total transmit power – Receiver sensitivity + gNB antenna gain (component 2). Definition of MIL (maximum isotropic loss): - MIL = Total transmit power – Receiver sensitivity gNB antenna gain (component 2 + 3 + 4) + UE antenna gain – Tx loss – Rx loss. Definition of MPL (Maximum Coupling Loss): - MPL = MIL – Shadow fading margin + BS selection/macro-diversity gain – Penetration margin + Other gains. Following agreement were reached in RAN1#102 meeting. Different metrics can be used for the coverage performance evaluation of different appropriate targets, the metrics used in R17 Coverage enhancement, R18 RedCap, R19 LP-WUS are summarized as following. Table 2: Metrics in different technologies study during NR Diverse use cases and device types are considered for the coverage target(s) identification. MCL metric does not take the impact of UE antenna gain into account, while MPL and MIL could reflect the differences in antenna gain of different UEs. Proposal 14: At least MIL or MPL can be used as the metric for the coverage target(s) identification when considering diverse use cases and device types. Link budget template The link budget template from Rel-17 NR coverage enhancement in TR 38.830 has been used to evaluate the coverage performance for several subsequent technologies, e.g., RedCap, LP-WUS. Its application across these diverse technologies demonstrates its robustness and generalizability. Thus we propose the link budget template from Rel-17 NR coverage enhancement in TR 38.830 can be used as a starting point. Proposal 15: Link budget template from Rel-17 NR coverage enhancement in TR 38.830 can be used as a starting point.
